Governance Note — Reformulation Excluded from Scope
During the Week 13 build, the Reformulation category (originally proposed as FLEX_01) was reviewed jointly with a second SME and excluded from the Linguistic Flexibility scaffold domain. The construct itself is valid — the capacity to self-monitor and pivot to a different formulation when one isn't working is a real ILR 3 flexibility marker — but Extended Supported Opinion's monologic, prepared-response structure does not reliably create the conditions where genuine reformulation occurs naturally. Attempting to elicit or score it within ESO risks rewarding performative self-correction rather than authentic communicative repair. The decision was to exclude it from the active four-category domain rather than build it with reduced confidence; it remains a candidate for a future task type with a more dialogic or interactive structure, where the elicitation conditions would be genuine.

Task 1 — Framework (Linguistic Flexibility, four categories)
This domain evaluates whether a speaker can do the same communicative work in more than one linguistic way — restructure a sentence, reshape a word, combine propositions, or represent a viewpoint other than their own — rather than relying on a single fixed pattern regardless of context. Where Discourse Frame asks "can you reason this way" and Grammar Structure asks "can you control this form," Flexibility asks "can you adapt when the situation calls for a different shape." Scope matches the existing two domains: ESO tasks, ILR 3 as target, ILR 2 as assumed floor, scaffold-selectable, non-scoring (holistic invariant applies identically — Flexibility selection changes feedback weighting only, never the holistic score).
ILR 2 ceiling behavior shows up as a single syntactic mold reused sentence after sentence, one fixed form of a content word regardless of grammatical slot, short independent clauses strung together rather than subordinated, and an inability to represent any viewpoint but the speaker's own. ILR 3 functional realization is the inverse of each: vary sentence shape across a response, shift word class to fit the slot, combine propositions into hierarchically organized sentences, and grammatically integrate someone else's stance into the response.
FLEX_01 — Structural Variation. The capacity to carry comparable content across more than one syntactic frame within a response — active/passive, fronting, cleft constructions, varied clause ordering — instead of defaulting to one habitual sentence template throughout.
FLEX_02 — Word Form Flexibility. The ability to move across word-class forms of a shared root (noun/verb/adjective/adverb) to fit the grammatical slot, rather than locking a key concept into one fixed form and working around it.
FLEX_03 — Sentence Combining. The ability to merge two or more simple propositions into a single complex or compound sentence using coordination, subordination, or embedding — producing connected, hierarchically organized speech rather than a string of short independent clauses.
FLEX_04 — Perspective Integration. The ability to incorporate another viewpoint — a counterargument, a third party's view, a hypothetical objector — into one's own discourse in a grammatically integrated way, rather than only ever speaking from a single first-person vantage point.
Boundary against the existing domains: Perspective Integration is not the same construct as the counterargument requirement already locked as a Score 4 holistic feature — that's argument structure, this is the grammatical mechanism for voicing a perspective. Same logic protects Sentence Combining against Grammar Structure's Relative & Embedded Clauses — Grammar asks "is this specific structure controlled," Flexibility asks "is there any combining happening across the response at all."
Confirmed architecture: Emerging/Developing/Sustained scale matching FRAME/STRUCT, feeds into the existing Focus Feedback box, forward-compatible with Combined mode eventually supporting a three-way Frame + Structure + Flexibility selection.
